    content: 'Climate change is arguably the most pressing existential threat of the 21st century, and its impact on global agriculture is particularly alarming. As average temperatures rise and weather patterns become increasingly erratic, the traditional models of farming are being severely disrupted.\n\nOne of the most direct consequences is the increased frequency of extreme weather events. Prolonged droughts can devastate crop yields, while sudden, intense floods can wash away fertile topsoil. Furthermore, shifting temperature zones are altering the geographic range of agricultural pests and diseases, exposing crops to new threats for which they have no natural resistance.\n\nWhile some regions in higher latitudes might temporarily benefit from longer growing seasons, the overall global effect is overwhelmingly negative. Staple crops like wheat, rice, and maize are highly sensitive to thermal stress. A failure in these harvests could trigger global food shortages and severe price spikes, disproportionately affecting vulnerable populations in developing nations. To mitigate these risks, scientists are urgently developing drought-resistant crop varieties and advocating for more resilient, sustainable agricultural practices.',
