    title: 'The Ethics of Artificial Intelligence',
    content: 'As Artificial Intelligence (AI) systems become increasingly integrated into daily life, profound ethical questions have emerged. AI is no longer confined to science fiction; algorithms currently approve bank loans, screen job applicants, and even assist in medical diagnoses. While these systems offer unprecedented efficiency, they also pose significant risks regarding fairness and bias.\n\nMachine learning models are trained on vast datasets of human behavior. If these historical datasets contain societal biases—such as racial or gender prejudice—the AI will inadvertently learn and perpetuate those prejudices. For instance, an AI recruitment tool trained on data from a male-dominated industry might automatically downgrade the resumes of female candidates.\n\nAnother major ethical concern is accountability. When an autonomous vehicle is involved in a fatal collision, or when an AI makes a disastrous medical recommendation, determining who is legally and morally responsible—the programmer, the user, or the machine itself—is a complex legal gray area. Consequently, there is a growing consensus among tech leaders and ethicists that robust regulatory frameworks and transparent AI development processes are urgently required.',
